<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shoshone</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shoshoni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shoshone are a Native American people of the Great Basin, the Snake River plain and the Rocky Mountains, speaking a Numic language of the Uto-Aztecan family. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> split from them around 1700 and moved onto the southern Plains. Sacagawea, who guided the Lewis and Clark expedition, was a Lemhi Shoshone, and Shoshone communities live today in Wyoming, Idaho, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shoshone.jpg" alt="Shoshone" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cyrus Thomas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shoshone_group_American_Indian_Mongoloid.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their ancestors were Shoshone-speaking bands of the Great Basin and the northern Plains in present-day Wyoming, and their language, of the Numic branch of the Uto-Aztecan family, became distinct from <b>Shoshone</b> around 1700.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nahuatl belongs to the Uto-Aztecan family, which stretches from the Great Basin of the western United States, where <b>Shoshone</b> and Comanche were spoken, through the Hopi of Arizona and the Yaqui and Huichol of northern and western Mexico, to its southernmost branch, the Nahuan languages of central Mexico…</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>California Volunteers under Colonel Patrick Edward Connor attacked a <b>Shoshone</b> winter camp in present-day Franklin County, Idaho, killing an estimated 250 to 493 people.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-29">29 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
